00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.950
I think that logging
exists from the beginning,

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:36.910
since if you're doing
development, you're debugging.

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:38.970
You have some kind
of logging mechanism.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:40.622
If you are deploying
your application,

00:06:40.622 --> 00:06:42.330
you have some logging
mechanism for that.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:46.420
It could be through syslog,
rsyslog, or just writing

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:49.700
to a plain log file
in the file system.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:52.290
But we have current
problems now.

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:55.560
In the past, we used to
have, like, common messages.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:07:00.060
The application is doing A,
B, C, D, or it's crashing.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:02.100
We have some exceptions.

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:06.360
But what happens when you have
multiple applications that

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:09.370
generate data in
a different way?

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:12.090
For example, if you
know Apache web server,

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:15.260
Apache web server creates
their own log entry,

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:17.130
saying this is an
IP address, this

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:20.880
is a practical version
size of the document.

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.210
If you look at, for
example, MySQL logs, they

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:25.960
look pretty much different.

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:28.700
What kind of query was running?

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:29.790
What's our reception?

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:31.500
How are the tables?

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.540
Do we have some charting
or things like that?

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:37.800
So if you want to
do logging, it's

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:39.160
not because logging is cool.

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:42.295
I think that, honestly,
logging is boring from a--

00:07:42.295 --> 00:07:43.170
MARY KOES: It's cool.

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:44.878
EDUARDO SILVA: --from
a play perspective,

00:07:44.878 --> 00:07:48.510
but you have to deal with
that, because you have problems

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:50.410
that you need to solve.

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:54.090
So the thing is if you have
multiple source of information

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:58.320
that come from different
places in different formats,

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.840
the ultimate goal that you
want to do is data analysis.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:05.850
So in order to do data analysis,
you need a logging agent,

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:11.070
and this logging agent must
have different strategies

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.980
to deal with data
formats, data sources.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:16.350
One thing is to read log
files from the file system.

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:19.260
The other is to listen
from TCP or UDP.

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:21.570
If you're using, for
example, hardware devices

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:25.110
like firewalls, and they are
sending their logs over UDP,

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:27.570
you need to support that too.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:29.970
So we are moving forward.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:32.449
We can think about
many logging agents,

00:08:32.449 --> 00:08:36.090
but the experience here is
that Treasure Data, the company

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:40.350
that I work for, we created
Fluentd around seven years ago

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:43.020
to solve the whole data
collection problem.

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:46.200
And Fluentd is an
open source project.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:47.880
It's fully open
source, and right now,

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.910
the CNCF, the Cloud Native
Computing Foundation,

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:54.630
the goal of this project is
it allows you to consume logs

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:58.200
from multiple places
with different formats

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:02.780
and ingest back the information
on any kind of central database

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:05.760
like Google Stackdriver
or your own elastic search

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:07.950
database or MySQL.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:10.410
In general, as a
project and ecosystem,

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:12.840
we have more than 700 plugins.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.730
And I think that from
a company perspective,

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:17.040
we maintain like 10 or 20.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:19.600
And all of the others are
made by the community.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:20.940
Do you know Fluentd?

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:23.050
Is anybody using
Fluentd before here?

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:23.550
Good.

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:24.380
We have some users.

00:09:27.729 --> 00:09:28.520
MARY KOES: Awesome.

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:29.350
Thanks.

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:33.070
So once we get logs into
the centralized logging

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:35.620
API, however that
happens, and many times

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:38.170
through our logging
agent, our next goal

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:40.310
is to get it to the
right destination.

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:43.340
And this is where the log
router comes into place.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:47.620
So we have two kind of
different flows for managing

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:49.810
logs through the log router.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:54.370
One is called log sinks, and
that is basically an inclusion

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:57.100
filter, so you're going to say
exactly what it is you want

00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:59.177
sent to these destinations.

00:09:59.177 --> 00:10:00.760
And if you don't say
anything, nothing

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:04.060
will go to these destinations.

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:07.110
We can send the same log
to BigQuery and Pub/Sub

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:09.250
or to none of the above.

00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:12.520
On the other side,
we have logs being

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:16.720
sent to Stackdriver,
the logging product

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:18.910
and we manage this
via exclusion filter.

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:20.770
So if you do nothing,
all your logs

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:23.200
will go to Stackdriver logging.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:26.080
And then if there are certain
logs that you don't want to go

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.750
there-- either because you don't
want anyone in your company

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:33.640
to have access to them or if
you don't want to pay for them--

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:36.370
then you can use
an exclusion filter

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:39.040
to control what goes
into Stackdriver logging.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:40.480
And that's a pretty
big difference

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:43.330
compared to many
of the other cloud

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:47.000
platform logging
experiences out there.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:52.420
So I'll call out two important
ideas around our log router.

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:56.110
One is that logs can be
exported to log sinks,

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:59.660
even if they are excluded
from Stackdriver log storage.

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:03.040
So this is something
that can be a little bit

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:05.290
tricky to get from our
documentation-- we'll work on

00:11:05.290 --> 00:11:08.350
that-- but hopefully, clear now.

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:11.170
And then the other thing
that's a really powerful tool

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:13.720
is a tool called
aggregated exports.

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:17.680
So most of our customers
have an organization

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:20.230
with more than one
project, and they

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:24.030
want to manage logs across
their entire organization.

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:27.550
A special use case here is
security, where oftentimes,

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:30.250
the security team wants
to see the audit logs

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:32.770
across their entire
organization.

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:35.710
And so an aggregated export can
be set up either at the folder

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:38.500
level or at the org level.

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:41.360
And it will inherit all
of the stuff below it,

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:44.920
so if audit logs are created
for a new project that

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:47.750
didn't exist now in
the organization,

00:11:47.750 --> 00:11:52.480
we will go ahead and include
those in the export as well.

00:11:55.670 --> 00:11:57.400
And then the last
thing I'll talk

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:59.410
about in terms of
logging tools is how you

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:01.030
analyze logs with Stackdriver.

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:03.430
And we'll get to a demo
here in just a moment,

00:12:03.430 --> 00:12:06.190
but Stackdriver Logging
supports both basic and advanced

00:12:06.190 --> 00:12:08.080
filters.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:09.970
We also have a tool
called Error Reporting

00:12:09.970 --> 00:12:13.000
as part of the logging
library toolkit

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:17.200
here that will automatically
go through your logs and group

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:20.980
like logs together, specifically
looking for stack traces

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:23.240
and issues in your code.

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:25.330
And then we also
provide the ability

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:28.940
to create alerts and
dashboards from logs

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:31.570
by using a tool called
Logs-Based Metrics,

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:33.929
and that pairs with
Stackdriver Monitoring.

00:12:33.929 --> 00:12:35.470
So that's kind of
a theme that you'll

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:37.990
hear across the
Stackdriver tools

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:39.880
is that while you can
use logging by itself

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:42.280
or monitoring by
itself or APM tools

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:46.210
by themselves, if you use
multiple ones together,

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:49.240
you can actually get something
that is more powerful than any

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:52.900
of them individually.

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:56.320
And now my favorite
part is demo time.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:04.000
If we could cut
over here, perfect.

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:09.660
All right, so I have a
web store running on GKE.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:14.730
And I can see the logs
from my web store.

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:18.270
At first glance, I can see, hey,
there are quite a bit of logs,

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:19.490
looks like a lot of errors.

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:20.990
But I'm not really
sure where to get

00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:23.680
started if there's an issue.

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:27.624
So my first stop and first
stop for many customers

00:13:27.624 --> 00:13:29.040
is let's check out
Error Reporting

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:32.890
to see if it's automatically
identified anything for us.

00:13:32.890 --> 00:13:36.090
And sure enough, having
looked through our logs,

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:40.890
out of all of those errors,
it's identified a specific error

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:42.990
in our application
that someone tried

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:45.150
to set the quantity
to less than zero,

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:47.770
which caused a specific error.

00:13:47.770 --> 00:13:49.140
And I can click on that.

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:52.080
I can see exactly how
many customers have

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:54.750
been impacted by this error.

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:56.970
I can see the stack
trace so that I

00:13:56.970 --> 00:14:00.450
can tell exactly where in
my code just came from,

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:03.420
and I could go to
Stackdriver Debugging

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:06.390
to investigate further,
if I wanted to, from here.

00:14:06.390 --> 00:14:12.320
I can also jump directly back to
the logs to view the raw logs.

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:15.770
I can link to an
issue, so this one here

00:14:15.770 --> 00:14:19.940
links to a GitHub
issue, for example.

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:22.920
And I can also track
the resolution status.

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:26.870
So for example, if someone tells
me one of our developers says,

00:14:26.870 --> 00:14:28.545
hey, I fixed this error.

00:14:28.545 --> 00:14:29.420
It should be all set.

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:30.500
It's deployed.

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:34.400
I can go ahead and say, this
error should be resolved,

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:37.100
and then we say,
OK, no known errors.

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:38.240
That's great.

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:42.740
If I go back to
my catalog here--

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:46.670
and we'll test it to see
if this actually works--

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:50.240
and I add something
to my cart, let's see

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:52.430
what happens if we try
to set the quantity

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:53.990
to a negative number.

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:56.690
We'll update the
basket, and I'll

00:14:56.690 --> 00:15:00.260
come back over to
Error Reporting,

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:02.330
and reload it here
for just a moment.

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:11.500
And this usually updates in
about five to 10 seconds,

00:15:11.500 --> 00:15:13.240
and we can see that
it automatically

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:16.440
identified that the error had
been seen again and reopened

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:18.010
to the issue.

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:20.620
If I wanted to, I can
also turn on notifications

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:22.600
so that rather than me
going to this dashboard,

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:26.830
it'll proactively push alerts
for new or re-opened errors

00:15:26.830 --> 00:15:30.210
to my inbox.

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:32.930
So that's a very
common use case we

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:34.700
hear from a lot
of developers who

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:37.010
are using Stackdriver Logging.

00:15:37.010 --> 00:15:39.920
But we hear a number
of other examples

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:43.290
for, like, security use cases.

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:46.940
So something that
I hear a lot is,

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:52.010
I want to be alerted if
anyone adds an email,

00:15:52.010 --> 00:15:56.420
let's say from a
Gmail.com domain.

00:15:56.420 --> 00:16:00.260
So in the UI, I can
interact with it

00:16:00.260 --> 00:16:02.330
in sort of a point
and click mode,

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:09.110
but I can also interact with
the logs in the Advanced Filter,

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:11.940
and then type in more
advanced queries.

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:13.940
So in the case of
identifying something

00:16:13.940 --> 00:16:17.330
that comes from a
Gmail.com account,

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:19.670
I need to create a logs-based
metric so that I can then

00:16:19.670 --> 00:16:20.580
alert on that.

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:23.240
So I'm going to go over
to my Logs-Based Metrics,

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:25.850
and I've created one here.

00:16:25.850 --> 00:16:28.310
I'm going to go ahead
and edit that metric.

00:16:28.310 --> 00:16:31.490
For anything that
comes from logs

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:38.390
that are set IAM policy, that
binds a member to @gmail.com,

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:39.740
I want to count all of these.

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:41.156
And that's kind
of the first step.

00:16:41.156 --> 00:16:43.820
So I'll be able to have
dashboards about everyone who's

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:46.820
been added from an
@gmail.com account,

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:49.620
and then I can also create
alerting policies on that.

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:54.650
And I can see that earlier
this morning, my Google account

00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:57.890
did add somebody
who was @gmail.com.

00:16:57.890 --> 00:17:01.160
So let me go back to
our logs-based metrics

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:04.470
and go ahead and show
you how you would create

00:17:04.470 --> 00:17:06.849
an alerting policy on this.

00:17:06.849 --> 00:17:09.460
So I'm going to Create
Alert from Metric.

00:17:09.460 --> 00:17:13.170
This will pop me over into
Stackdriver Monitoring, which

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:15.630
is where I manage
all of my dashboards

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:18.020
and my alerting policies.

00:17:18.020 --> 00:17:24.900
So I'm able to, here, see
the logs-based metric.

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:25.950
Take a look here.

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:28.530
I don't have any
recent ones, but I'm

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:32.850
going to go ahead and create
an alerting policy if I ever

00:17:32.850 --> 00:17:37.560
see this, because I don't
ever expect to see this.

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:41.040
And instead of duration, I'm
going to say most recent value.

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:44.070
So if this ever happens,
I want to be alerted.

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:48.400
Go ahead and save the condition,
and create a notification.

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:51.480
So I'll send it to my
favorite email address,

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:53.100
auditlogsrock@gmail.com.

00:17:55.910 --> 00:18:00.440
I can add some documentation
in terms of who to contact.

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:01.790
Name this policy.

00:18:01.790 --> 00:18:03.480
And I'll go ahead and save it.

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:09.650
So now if I go ahead and add
a new member to an IAM policy

00:18:09.650 --> 00:18:11.810
anywhere in this
project, I will receive

00:18:11.810 --> 00:18:13.220
a notification about this.

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:20.260
Another common use case
we hear from customers

00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:23.530
is that they want to send
their logs someplace else.

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:26.920
So this is the beauty of the
centralized logging solution

00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:29.050
is that it all
comes in centrally,

00:18:29.050 --> 00:18:31.480
and then we can slice
and dice it and send it

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:34.210
to many different places
using exports, which

00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:36.280
is the log router
that we were talking

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:38.660
about just a moment ago.

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:41.680
So I'll start out
with a very common use

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:44.770
case, which is I want to
send all of my audit logs

00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:46.480
to BigQuery.

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:49.210
So I select a filter.

00:18:49.210 --> 00:18:51.010
In this case, I'm
going to say everything

00:18:51.010 --> 00:18:54.790
that matches the activity
audit logs in the log name.

00:18:54.790 --> 00:18:59.500
And then I simply select
BigQuery and the destination

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:01.390
and create a sink.

00:19:01.390 --> 00:19:04.930
And any future logs that
match this will automatically

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:08.260
be sent to BigQuery,
which is great,

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:10.330
but that only helps
me for this project.

00:19:10.330 --> 00:19:14.300
What if I want to do this
at the organization level?

00:19:14.300 --> 00:19:20.350
So in this case, I need to
pull up my Cloud Shell here.

00:19:20.350 --> 00:19:25.170
And I can use a gcloud
command to set this

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:27.210
at the organizational level.

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:29.200
And I'll call out right here.

00:19:29.200 --> 00:19:32.470
We have "include children,"
and we're setting this up

00:19:32.470 --> 00:19:34.366
at the organization level.

00:19:34.366 --> 00:19:35.740
Same thing, though--
the log name

00:19:35.740 --> 00:19:38.920
matches the filter
of anything that

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:42.330
has cloudaudit.googleapis.com.

00:19:42.330 --> 00:19:45.020
And I'm going to go
ahead and save this,

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:47.110
and now any audit
logs from anywhere

00:19:47.110 --> 00:19:50.800
in my organization will
all go to the same BigQuery

00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:52.310
destination.

00:19:52.310 --> 00:19:54.070
Now one tricky thing
just to remember

00:19:54.070 --> 00:19:56.440
is now that I set it
up at the org level,

00:19:56.440 --> 00:19:59.260
any audit logs that
come from this project

00:19:59.260 --> 00:20:00.304
will be written twice.

00:20:00.304 --> 00:20:01.720
So in that case,
I'd probably want

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:08.550
to go back and get rid of
the one at the project level.

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:10.770
Another thing that
we have users do

00:20:10.770 --> 00:20:15.370
a lot is I want to act on logs.

00:20:15.370 --> 00:20:19.530
So in this case, let's say
every time a new VM is spun up,

00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:21.870
I want to take a look
at it, process it

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:25.050
with Cloud Functions,
maybe, and add some labels

00:20:25.050 --> 00:20:28.840
or apply some rules to it.

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:32.970
So I'm going to create
a sink for any time

00:20:32.970 --> 00:20:36.965
that I have created an instance,
which is the insert command.

00:20:40.670 --> 00:20:43.940
And I'll be able to send
all of these to Pub/Sub.

00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:47.150
I can then use a Cloud Function
to pull all of the logs

00:20:47.150 --> 00:20:52.150
from Pub/Sub, process them, take
whatever action I want on them.

00:20:52.150 --> 00:20:56.260
So this is another
common use case we see.

00:20:56.260 --> 00:20:59.460
And then last but
not least, we'll

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:02.390
talk about log exclusions.

00:21:02.390 --> 00:21:05.860
So we have a page
dedicated to helping

00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:08.710
you understand what
your log volume is

00:21:08.710 --> 00:21:14.170
across your various Google
Cloud resources or AWS resources

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:16.550
here as well.

00:21:16.550 --> 00:21:20.410
And I can see, for example,
taking a look here,

00:21:20.410 --> 00:21:23.695
a lot of my volume
in this project

00:21:23.695 --> 00:21:30.790
is coming from Kubernetes, so
that's the bulk of my logs.

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:32.610
I can see, though,
that projected

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:36.280
through the end of the month
is 43 gigabytes, which is well

00:21:36.280 --> 00:21:39.730
within the free limit
of 50 gigabytes,

00:21:39.730 --> 00:21:41.650
so I'm not too, too worried.

00:21:41.650 --> 00:21:44.410
But I could go ahead
and say, you know what?

00:21:44.410 --> 00:21:47.380
I'm going to send these
logs maybe to my Elk Stack.

00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:50.230
I don't want to pay for
them in Stackdriver.

00:21:50.230 --> 00:21:53.170
I could go ahead and disable
the logs altogether here,

00:21:53.170 --> 00:21:55.210
or I could create an
exclusion filter based

00:21:55.210 --> 00:22:02.050
on this and, for example,
say, anything that is less

00:22:02.050 --> 00:22:06.520
than a warning level, I want to
maybe just sample those logs.

00:22:06.520 --> 00:22:10.000
So instead of
excluding 100% of them,

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:14.470
maybe I will set this is 99%.

00:22:14.470 --> 00:22:19.560
I can also deep dive into the
logs volume in Stackdriver

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:23.460
Monitoring using the
Metrics Explorer tool

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:27.180
and visualize exactly where my
volume of logs is coming from.

00:22:30.950 --> 00:22:36.511
And if we could cut back please
to the presentation, awesome.

00:22:42.979 --> 00:22:45.270
EDUARDO SILVA: So now with
a solution like Stackdriver,

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:47.970
we can say that logging
is not long and boring.

00:22:47.970 --> 00:22:52.080
But before that, handling
logs in the different formats,

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:56.100
many sources, and correlate
data is quite hard.

00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:59.910
And I'm sure if you are working
already with distributed system

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:02.910
or cluster or with
Kubernetes, there's

00:23:02.910 --> 00:23:06.010
quite challenges that
needs to be solved.

00:23:06.010 --> 00:23:07.950
So what I'm going
to explain now is

00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:12.460
about how logging operates
at Kubernetes level.

00:23:12.460 --> 00:23:15.000
So if you understand
the problem, how

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:17.280
it works behind
the scenes, means

00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:19.500
you can optimize
the engine queries

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:22.470
and get better insights
from your data.

00:23:22.470 --> 00:23:25.940
Do we have any
Kubernetes users here?

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:28.740
Oh, good.

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:32.610
So I'm going to do a
little introduction

00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:35.130
about how logging
works in Kubernetes

00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:37.200
or when you play with
docking containers.

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:39.870
And basically, you
have one application

00:23:39.870 --> 00:23:42.720
that triggers a message,
and that message

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:47.850
means to be like a log message
saying a status, an alert,

00:23:47.850 --> 00:23:49.950
a warning, or anything related.

00:23:49.950 --> 00:23:52.710
For example, we have,
like, "Hey Next."

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:55.290
But it's not just
about the message.

00:23:55.290 --> 00:23:59.460
That message has some metadata
that needs to be correlated.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:03.600
One of them is at what time
this message was generated,

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:09.600
and the other is what's the
channel that this message was

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:10.980
generated from.

00:24:10.980 --> 00:24:13.440
If we speak about
containers, there

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:16.970
are two main channels, standard
output, standard error.

00:24:16.970 --> 00:24:20.355
And here, for example, "Hey
Next" is just not a next.

00:24:20.355 --> 00:24:24.770
We have JSON example
with metadata.

00:24:24.770 --> 00:24:29.640
That message then needs
to be stored somewhere.

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:31.580
There are many ways to
store container logs

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:34.470
or in Kubernetes logs, if,
for example, we have systemd.

00:24:34.470 --> 00:24:37.890
But here we are going to
refer how Docker operates.

00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:41.100
Basically, your message is
stored in the file system,

00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:42.750
in a pod.

00:24:42.750 --> 00:24:45.960
So every message
becomes a new JSON map,

00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:50.550
and every message is appended
at the end of that file.

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:54.810
But things will become a
little bit complex later,

00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:57.090
because if we think
about how Kubernetes

00:24:57.090 --> 00:24:59.800
works from an
architectural perspective,

00:24:59.800 --> 00:25:01.020
we can think about this.

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:05.220
You have your application,
the most simple use case.

00:25:05.220 --> 00:25:07.980
That application is
running in a container.

00:25:07.980 --> 00:25:11.100
Container appends
limitation restrictions

00:25:11.100 --> 00:25:14.370
and allows you to set
up certain policy rules

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:18.600
and also how this
process that is running

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:20.732
can communicate with others.

00:25:20.732 --> 00:25:22.190
And when I say the
"container," you

00:25:22.190 --> 00:25:24.570
know that container is
just a concept, right?

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:26.350
From an operating
system perspective,

00:25:26.350 --> 00:25:30.850
it's all about
namespaces and cgroups.

00:25:30.850 --> 00:25:33.070
So your application
runs in a container,

00:25:33.070 --> 00:25:38.070
and that container runs in a
concept, which is called a pod.

00:25:38.070 --> 00:25:40.620
So things get
complex, because a pod

00:25:40.620 --> 00:25:44.330
can have multiple containers.

00:25:44.330 --> 00:25:48.270
Multiple pods can be
running on the same node.

00:25:48.270 --> 00:25:50.360
And a node I'm referring
to to a bare metal

00:25:50.360 --> 00:25:53.190
machine or a BPEL machine.

00:25:53.190 --> 00:25:57.300
So here is just one single
machine, but in a real cluster,

00:25:57.300 --> 00:25:59.460
you have many of them.

00:25:59.460 --> 00:26:02.180
So imagine that you have
your distributed application.

00:26:02.180 --> 00:26:05.230
You told Kubernetes, please
deploy my application.

00:26:05.230 --> 00:26:07.650
Kubernetes, based on
the scaling policies,

00:26:07.650 --> 00:26:11.480
is going to decide where
these containers will run

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:12.510
or will scale up.

00:26:12.510 --> 00:26:14.930
It's going to create
some replicas on which

00:26:14.930 --> 00:26:17.360
node these replicas will run.

00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:22.130
And likely, not all of them
will run in the same node.

00:26:22.130 --> 00:26:23.660
So these become complex.

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:26.900
If I tell you 20
years ago, please

00:26:26.900 --> 00:26:29.840
look at my logs from
my application, well,

00:26:29.840 --> 00:26:31.030
you open the terminal.

00:26:31.030 --> 00:26:34.700
You do some SSH and look what's
going on with your application.

00:26:34.700 --> 00:26:37.400
Just cut, file, and
you get the message.

00:26:37.400 --> 00:26:40.070
But on this, if you
have a huge cluster,

00:26:40.070 --> 00:26:44.390
you cannot do an SSH on every
node and try to find which is

00:26:44.390 --> 00:26:47.960
the local JSON file that belongs
to the application that I just

00:26:47.960 --> 00:26:50.780
deployed, but maybe that
application will destroy it,

00:26:50.780 --> 00:26:54.650
because it failed
or it was scaled up.

00:26:54.650 --> 00:26:58.820
So things become more
complex, and complex

00:26:58.820 --> 00:27:02.700
because we need to create or
try to gather some context

00:27:02.700 --> 00:27:05.960
and what we refer with context,
because your application is

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:06.900
running in a pod.

00:27:06.900 --> 00:27:08.090
That pod has a name.

00:27:08.090 --> 00:27:11.110
That pod has an ID,
an identification.

00:27:11.110 --> 00:27:13.220
Also, you're running
in a namespace.

00:27:13.220 --> 00:27:15.960
If you deploy a pod, that
pod belongs to a namespace.

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:18.290
It's running in a
node, in a host.

00:27:18.290 --> 00:27:22.700
Maybe you added some labels
and annotation to the pod,

00:27:22.700 --> 00:27:26.030
because maybe an
annotation say, no--

00:27:26.030 --> 00:27:27.510
this-- for example--

00:27:27.510 --> 00:27:28.010
sorry.

00:27:28.010 --> 00:27:31.380
Some labels saying,
OK, [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:31.380 --> 00:27:35.180
running or belongs to
production, quality, testing,

00:27:35.180 --> 00:27:36.840
or anything like that.

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:39.080
So how do you create
all this context

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:43.010
for an application which
is running in Kubernetes?

00:27:43.010 --> 00:27:47.700
This whole context also
[INAUDIBLE] more complexity.

00:27:47.700 --> 00:27:48.690
And it's because this.

00:27:48.690 --> 00:27:51.080
Because the container
name and the container ID,

00:27:51.080 --> 00:27:53.250
you can find it in
the file system.

00:27:53.250 --> 00:27:55.290
And I'm talking here
about the very low level

00:27:55.290 --> 00:27:58.260
how things operate.

00:27:58.260 --> 00:27:59.350
You can get that.

00:27:59.350 --> 00:28:03.480
But all of the other components
come from the Kubernetes API

00:28:03.480 --> 00:28:06.000
server or the master server.

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:08.270
So you have some
information that is locally

00:28:08.270 --> 00:28:10.620
in the node and other
information that's

00:28:10.620 --> 00:28:13.630
in a separate machine.

00:28:13.630 --> 00:28:16.710
And you need that
your logging agent

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:21.510
be able to get all of
this information together.

00:28:21.510 --> 00:28:24.240
And that's why the logging
agent that you use and that

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:28.470
Stackdriver use needs to be able
to handle all of this for you,

00:28:28.470 --> 00:28:31.470
because of course, you
cannot do this manually.

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:35.010
But your logging agent needs
to be able to create a file

00:28:35.010 --> 00:28:38.400
system, understand the
messages, parse the messages--

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:40.080
which are in different formats--

00:28:40.080 --> 00:28:42.360
and correlate all the
information together

00:28:42.360 --> 00:28:45.760
with context coming
from the API server.

00:28:45.760 --> 00:28:47.280
And that's Fluentd.

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:49.550
Actually, Stackdriver
uses Fluentd.

00:28:49.550 --> 00:28:51.830
You will find it
as Google Fluentd,

00:28:51.830 --> 00:28:53.520
because it's a
Fluentd package that's

00:28:53.520 --> 00:28:55.980
ready to use with
Google services, which

00:28:55.980 --> 00:28:58.320
has the whole extensions
and integration

00:28:58.320 --> 00:29:02.300
with the Stackdriver
right out of the box.

00:29:02.300 --> 00:29:06.480
And once you get the things
of Fluent as a log processor,

00:29:06.480 --> 00:29:09.060
you can send things to
Stackdriver or maybe

00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:10.080
your own database.

00:29:10.080 --> 00:29:13.620
Maybe you're running on prem,
or you have your own system

00:29:13.620 --> 00:29:14.760
to store logs.

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:17.430
So that's why the
logging agents--

00:29:17.430 --> 00:29:20.610
it's not easy to be a
logging agent as a tool,

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:23.670
and also needs to be
able and communicate

00:29:23.670 --> 00:29:27.560
with different components
over the network.

00:29:27.560 --> 00:29:33.160
Now log processing in Kubernetes
is like the next step.

00:29:33.160 --> 00:29:37.360
If we think that we
have just a single node,

00:29:37.360 --> 00:29:39.970
well, we'll have the
master and a single node.

00:29:39.970 --> 00:29:45.350
Fluentd, as a logging agent,
needs to run as a DaemonSet.

00:29:45.350 --> 00:29:48.160
Do you know what a DaemonSet is?

00:29:48.160 --> 00:29:49.727
Are you familiar with pods?

00:29:49.727 --> 00:29:50.560
We just spoke about.

00:29:50.560 --> 00:29:51.360
OK.

00:29:51.360 --> 00:29:54.340
A DaemonSet-- it's like
a pod, but this pod

00:29:54.340 --> 00:29:57.590
runs on every node
of the cluster.

00:29:57.590 --> 00:30:00.160
So if you append more nodes
to your Kubernetes cluster,

00:30:00.160 --> 00:30:03.520
like a [INAUDIBLE] machine,
and you already deploy it,

00:30:03.520 --> 00:30:07.720
a DaemonSet, you're going to get
a new pod with that DaemonSet

00:30:07.720 --> 00:30:09.700
running on that new node.

00:30:09.700 --> 00:30:13.240
So the way to work is that you
deploy Fluentd as a DaemonSet.

00:30:13.240 --> 00:30:15.670
Of course,
[INAUDIBLE] is already

00:30:15.670 --> 00:30:20.330
working by default. What
it does, it's running,

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:23.910
and it starts reading the whole
log files that we explained

00:30:23.910 --> 00:30:28.240
a few slides ago, and
all the content and JSON

00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:30.160
files that were created.

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:33.490
Once it read the whole
content from the application,

00:30:33.490 --> 00:30:36.880
it makes sure to gather
the metadata that

00:30:36.880 --> 00:30:40.040
agitate every message,
with every container,

00:30:40.040 --> 00:30:44.610
with every port, with labels,
annotations, and so on.

00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:49.270
So the simple message that
we started at the beginning

00:30:49.270 --> 00:30:52.340
becomes something like
this with context.

00:30:52.340 --> 00:30:55.870
Because it has Kubernetes, it
has a host, pod name, pod ID,

00:30:55.870 --> 00:30:58.460
container, name,
namespace, and so on,

00:30:58.460 --> 00:31:03.270
because then in a Stackdriver or
any database that you're using,

00:31:03.270 --> 00:31:07.430
you don't want to query a
show me the message that start

00:31:07.430 --> 00:31:09.080
with "Hey Next."

00:31:09.080 --> 00:31:12.170
You want to say, show me
all the messages that,

00:31:12.170 --> 00:31:14.660
for example, that the
container name, so

00:31:14.660 --> 00:31:18.290
Kubernetes, that container
name, equal "next."

00:31:18.290 --> 00:31:23.220
So this metadata allows you
to do a better data analysis

00:31:23.220 --> 00:31:27.080
and help the storage solution to
create the data that you really

00:31:27.080 --> 00:31:29.330
care about it and not
query all of them.

00:31:33.470 --> 00:31:34.390
MARY KOES: Thanks.

00:31:34.390 --> 00:31:40.670
So I mentioned earlier that
we have the same team that

00:31:40.670 --> 00:31:44.300
supports both our Cloud
Logging solution and Google's

00:31:44.300 --> 00:31:46.320
internal logging solution.

00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:48.050
So we've learned
a lot of lessons

00:31:48.050 --> 00:31:51.500
talking to customers like
yourselves and customers

00:31:51.500 --> 00:31:52.280
at Google--

00:31:52.280 --> 00:31:57.390
who use the logging
tools that we build.

00:31:57.390 --> 00:32:00.170
So I wanted to share
some lessons learned.

00:32:00.170 --> 00:32:03.530
One is the value
of structured logs,

00:32:03.530 --> 00:32:06.650
so not all logs
are created equal.

00:32:06.650 --> 00:32:10.160
They all have great potential,
but if we format them

00:32:10.160 --> 00:32:15.060
in a specific way, we're able
to answer questions more easily.

00:32:15.060 --> 00:32:16.970
So an unstructured
log might have

00:32:16.970 --> 00:32:22.100
a text payload of blah, blah,
blah, purchased four widgets.

00:32:22.100 --> 00:32:26.120
A structured log will
have a lot of key value

00:32:26.120 --> 00:32:28.550
pairs in JSON format.

00:32:28.550 --> 00:32:32.360
This makes it much easier,
then, to answer questions like,

00:32:32.360 --> 00:32:36.110
how many unique customers
purchased a widget?

00:32:36.110 --> 00:32:38.510
How many widgets were
purchased overall?

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:41.690
If you use structured
logs, you can then send--

00:32:41.690 --> 00:32:45.680
when you, for example,
send stuff to BigQuery,

00:32:45.680 --> 00:32:48.380
that structure will
persist and make

00:32:48.380 --> 00:32:51.960
it much easier to interact with
your logs downstream as well.

00:32:51.960 --> 00:32:54.770
Plus, you can do things in
Stackdriver logging like say,

00:32:54.770 --> 00:32:56.790
show me the customer ID hash.

00:32:56.790 --> 00:32:58.520
I want that to be
displayed easily,

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:03.230
so I can understand what's
going on more quickly.

00:33:03.230 --> 00:33:06.380
The other tip that we have
learned over the years

00:33:06.380 --> 00:33:09.140
is that it can be
really tricky to get

00:33:09.140 --> 00:33:10.940
the right level of logging.

00:33:10.940 --> 00:33:13.790
If you do too much,
it's crazy expensive,

00:33:13.790 --> 00:33:16.010
and you can't find
anything in your logs.

00:33:16.010 --> 00:33:18.110
If you do too little, then
you don't have the data

00:33:18.110 --> 00:33:19.830
that you need when you need it.

00:33:19.830 --> 00:33:23.090
And so trying to get that
just right level of logs

00:33:23.090 --> 00:33:26.000
can be a real challenge
both internal to Google

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:28.650
and for our customers.

00:33:28.650 --> 00:33:30.770
There's no magic bullet
here, but one thing

00:33:30.770 --> 00:33:34.040
we found to be really
helpful is to enforce

00:33:34.040 --> 00:33:38.540
a consistent definition
for logging levels

00:33:38.540 --> 00:33:40.340
through your code.

00:33:40.340 --> 00:33:44.210
So an error should be something
that really stopped your code,

00:33:44.210 --> 00:33:48.380
and you expect somebody to
actually investigate here,

00:33:48.380 --> 00:33:51.410
whereas maybe debug and
info are less important.

00:33:51.410 --> 00:33:53.510
And that makes it
easier, when I filter,

00:33:53.510 --> 00:33:59.410
to quickly find the
information that we care about.

00:33:59.410 --> 00:34:03.670
And the final kind of insight
we've derived over time

00:34:03.670 --> 00:34:08.110
is that it's really helpful to
have data about the log volume

00:34:08.110 --> 00:34:11.179
so that you can iterate
and refine this over time.

00:34:11.179 --> 00:34:14.090
So specifically, in
Stackdriver Logging,

00:34:14.090 --> 00:34:18.219
we have metrics at a
very fine-grained level,

00:34:18.219 --> 00:34:21.550
so you can see not only which
resources are sending logs,

00:34:21.550 --> 00:34:23.759
but which logs or which--

00:34:23.759 --> 00:34:26.050
are you getting mostly errors,
or are these mostly info

00:34:26.050 --> 00:34:27.054
statements?

00:34:27.054 --> 00:34:29.110
And you can slice
and dice your data

00:34:29.110 --> 00:34:31.300
in all sorts of
ways, which can allow

00:34:31.300 --> 00:34:34.510
you to iterate on your
logging solution over time.

00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:44.630
EDUARDO SILVA: So getting
back to logging agents,

00:34:44.630 --> 00:34:48.949
there are many solutions and
open source products available.

00:34:48.949 --> 00:34:53.570
But when talking
specifically about Fluentd,

00:34:53.570 --> 00:34:56.449
which is the default agent
in Stackdriver and also

00:34:56.449 --> 00:35:00.580
for [INAUDIBLE]
companies and products,

00:35:00.580 --> 00:35:03.080
we can think that
Fluentd is more than just

00:35:03.080 --> 00:35:05.400
a simple open source project.

00:35:05.400 --> 00:35:07.810
I would like to say that
it is a whole ecosystem.

00:35:07.810 --> 00:35:10.700
Since the moment there are many
companies contributing back

00:35:10.700 --> 00:35:14.150
to the same project
means that it's

00:35:14.150 --> 00:35:17.210
more relevant for the
market, and also, it's

00:35:17.210 --> 00:35:19.250
becoming a huge solution.

00:35:19.250 --> 00:35:24.340
And at some point, people said
that Fluentd for some cases

00:35:24.340 --> 00:35:27.660
was too heavy, or we have
some performance issues.

00:35:27.660 --> 00:35:33.880
And sometimes the question
is, how many records are you

00:35:33.880 --> 00:35:35.420
ingesting per second?

00:35:35.420 --> 00:35:37.482
Sometimes they say, a
few thousands per second.

00:35:37.482 --> 00:35:39.940
Of course, you're going to have
some performance penalties.

00:35:39.940 --> 00:35:42.550
You need memory, CPU, and
you are filtering data,

00:35:42.550 --> 00:35:46.630
and you have like 10 filters,
that is time-consuming.

00:35:46.630 --> 00:35:50.020
And they said, OK, but we
need something lightweight.

00:35:50.020 --> 00:35:53.520
Fluentd original is
written in Ruby and C.

00:35:53.520 --> 00:35:56.010
It's quite performant,
but at high scales,

00:35:56.010 --> 00:35:58.990
some companies
and user customers

00:35:58.990 --> 00:36:02.320
said we need something that
can be lightweight and more

00:36:02.320 --> 00:36:04.960
tied to cloud native solutions.

00:36:04.960 --> 00:36:07.810
And that's why we created
a second project, which

00:36:07.810 --> 00:36:12.310
is called Fluentbit, created
same by Treasure Data, now

00:36:12.310 --> 00:36:14.030
driven by the community.

00:36:14.030 --> 00:36:17.420
It's also with the Cloud
Native Computing Foundation,

00:36:17.420 --> 00:36:21.580
and Fluentbit tried to solve
the problems in the cloud native

00:36:21.580 --> 00:36:23.110
space in a different way.

00:36:23.110 --> 00:36:28.210
You've seen the same background
or experience from Fluentd.

00:36:28.210 --> 00:36:31.360
This is not like a
replacement for Fluentd,

00:36:31.360 --> 00:36:35.020
but most of cases, people
said, I want to use Fluentd.

00:36:35.020 --> 00:36:37.570
And for this special
namespace class,

00:36:37.570 --> 00:36:39.730
I'm going to run Fluentbit
because of performance

00:36:39.730 --> 00:36:42.230
reason or special features.

00:36:42.230 --> 00:36:44.380
The good thing about
Fluentbit is that it's

00:36:44.380 --> 00:36:47.620
written in Perl and C language.

00:36:47.620 --> 00:36:50.865
It's optimized for performance,
low memory footprint.

00:36:50.865 --> 00:36:54.310
And it also has a
pluggable architecture.

00:36:54.310 --> 00:36:57.340
It's pretty much a Fluentd,
but Fluentd ecosystem

00:36:57.340 --> 00:36:58.270
is quite huge.

00:36:58.270 --> 00:37:00.130
We have 700 plug-ins.

00:37:00.130 --> 00:37:02.440
Here, we have like 45.

00:37:02.440 --> 00:37:06.220
So you can see that it's more
tied for specific things,

00:37:06.220 --> 00:37:09.730
and it's fully integrated with
Kubernetes and Prometheus,

00:37:09.730 --> 00:37:13.340
and it's a sub-project
of the CNCF.

00:37:13.340 --> 00:37:15.990
And like a kind of
announcement, we

00:37:15.990 --> 00:37:19.080
are releasing in a couple of
days the new version, which

00:37:19.080 --> 00:37:20.840
is called 0.14.

00:37:20.840 --> 00:37:24.030
I think that sometimes numbers
does not mean something.

00:37:24.030 --> 00:37:27.420
The [INAUDIBLE] been around
for more than three years,

00:37:27.420 --> 00:37:30.660
but we're trying to have the
1.0 at the end of the year.

00:37:30.660 --> 00:37:36.160
And 0.14 will have integration
with Google Stackdriver.

00:37:36.160 --> 00:37:39.540
It will not, at the
beginning, this integration

00:37:39.540 --> 00:37:41.010
is quite experimental.

00:37:41.010 --> 00:37:43.800
It means that it doesn't
have the whole features

00:37:43.800 --> 00:37:47.040
that Google Fluentd has,
but we're working on that.

00:37:47.040 --> 00:37:50.190
Also, we will have the load
balancing capabilities.

00:37:50.190 --> 00:37:52.830
In logging, there is
one specific feature

00:37:52.830 --> 00:37:56.220
that if you are dispatching
your logs to your service,

00:37:56.220 --> 00:37:59.110
and you have some
[INAUDIBLE] audit,

00:37:59.110 --> 00:38:01.690
your logging agent needs
to be able to recover

00:38:01.690 --> 00:38:04.510
from that situation
meaning doing buffering

00:38:04.510 --> 00:38:07.600
or maybe trying, if
it fell from point A,

00:38:07.600 --> 00:38:09.260
try to do some
balancing, and try

00:38:09.260 --> 00:38:11.260
point B or a different node.

00:38:11.260 --> 00:38:14.450
So that balancing capability
is coming up right now,

00:38:14.450 --> 00:38:16.450
and also, we're
happy to say that we

00:38:16.450 --> 00:38:21.040
are implementing a new way to
do filtering in a logging agent.

00:38:21.040 --> 00:38:23.290
If you wanted to do some
filtering of Fluentd,

00:38:23.290 --> 00:38:26.800
you had to write your own
Ruby script for filtering.

00:38:26.800 --> 00:38:29.980
But if you wanted to do it
on Fluentbit, like few weeks

00:38:29.980 --> 00:38:33.730
ago, you had to
write your C filter.

00:38:33.730 --> 00:38:35.860
And for some people
said, I cannot write C.

00:38:35.860 --> 00:38:40.540
It's too complex, and I don't
have the time, which is right.

00:38:40.540 --> 00:38:43.940
We went in Europe at the
beginning of this year in QCon,

00:38:43.940 --> 00:38:47.800
and some people say,
hey, GDPR is coming.

00:38:47.800 --> 00:38:50.030
And I was not aware about GDPR.

00:38:50.030 --> 00:38:51.550
OK, what is GDPR?

00:38:51.550 --> 00:38:53.320
Explain it to me more about it.

00:38:53.320 --> 00:38:55.630
And they said, OK, we
need to obfuscate data.

00:38:55.630 --> 00:38:58.010
We need to add some conditionals
for the configuration

00:38:58.010 --> 00:39:02.630
for Fluentbit and fix like A, B,
C, D. OK, that is too complex.

00:39:02.630 --> 00:39:05.770
We cannot maintain multiple
plugins for different needs.

00:39:05.770 --> 00:39:09.905
Yeah, because they said our logs
are credit card transactions,

00:39:09.905 --> 00:39:11.530
and they have the
whole numbers, and we

00:39:11.530 --> 00:39:13.470
need to obfuscate the data.

00:39:13.470 --> 00:39:15.690
So we need a solution.

00:39:15.690 --> 00:39:17.170
That is important.

00:39:17.170 --> 00:39:20.500
So we said, OK, a new way
to do filtering will be,

00:39:20.500 --> 00:39:22.450
let's try to do some scripting.

00:39:22.450 --> 00:39:26.160
You run Fluentbit as an agent,
but also, when it starts,

00:39:26.160 --> 00:39:31.560
you can start your own scripts,
reading in Lua language, which

00:39:31.560 --> 00:39:33.270
are quite performant.

00:39:33.270 --> 00:39:36.090
And you can do all
the data modifications

00:39:36.090 --> 00:39:41.040
that you want without
low performance penalty.

00:39:41.040 --> 00:39:44.460
And also, we have
an address here.

00:39:44.460 --> 00:39:48.210
If you want to get more about
all the news on Fluentbit 0.14

00:39:48.210 --> 00:39:51.840
to be released, there's a
link on the slides that you--

00:39:51.840 --> 00:39:54.810
a form where you can
sign up to get the news.

00:39:54.810 --> 00:39:57.450
And we're taking
the Lua script--

00:39:57.450 --> 00:40:00.540
this is like a simple plugin.

00:40:00.540 --> 00:40:02.280
Well, this is like a text file.

00:40:02.280 --> 00:40:04.620
This is Lua, where
basically, you

00:40:04.620 --> 00:40:08.000
create a new record
using Lua tables,

00:40:08.000 --> 00:40:12.090
where you can put the
old content in one index

00:40:12.090 --> 00:40:14.190
and the new content
in a new key,

00:40:14.190 --> 00:40:16.480
and just work on those values.

00:40:16.480 --> 00:40:18.510
Fluentbit will be
able to understand

00:40:18.510 --> 00:40:21.270
that this modification
was requested from Lua,

00:40:21.270 --> 00:40:23.250
and it will modify
your records for you,

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:27.690
so you don't need to create
a new filter for Fluentbit,

00:40:27.690 --> 00:40:30.620
and just write your
own Lua scripts.

00:40:30.620 --> 00:40:33.600
So Fluentbit quite
exciting project.

00:40:33.600 --> 00:40:36.660
Actually, we get, like,
from a Docker and Kubernetes

00:40:36.660 --> 00:40:41.560
perspective, we get like 50,000
[INAUDIBLE] a day and growing,

00:40:41.560 --> 00:40:44.790
which is a lot, so we expect
to reach a huge millions

00:40:44.790 --> 00:40:47.960
at the end of the year.

00:40:47.960 --> 00:40:49.460
Thank you.

00:40:49.460 --> 00:40:50.960
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:50.960 --> 00:40:54.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

